WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:12.584
CS702 phase two prototype. Interactive Debugging and Steering of Multi-Agent AI Systems: re-implementing AGDebugger from CHI 2025. Team: Nguyen Nhat Minh and Do Duc Anh.

2
00:00:13.516 --> 00:00:26.891
The problem: teams of LLM agents exchange dozens of messages to solve a task. One wrong step cascades, and a long run ends with a wrong answer or none. Logs do not show where it went wrong, or let the developer try a fix without starting over.

3
00:00:27.803 --> 00:00:44.627
Target users: developers building and tuning multi-agent systems. Goal: find the message that derailed a run and steer the team to the correct result. Tasks: browse and step a long history, send messages, reset to an earlier point and edit the offending message, and keep an overview of the branches.

4
00:00:45.543 --> 00:01:10.285
Our prototype re-implements the paper's three components: C1, message history with sending and execution control; C2, reset-and-edit with session forking on real checkpoints; and C3, an overview visualisation. Own implementation on AutoGen 0.7.5 and React. The team under debug: a Magentic-One orchestrator, Coder, Executor and WebSearcher on Claude Haiku 5.5.

5
00:01:29.732 --> 00:01:35.272
A recorded failing run is loaded, replayed from saved history and checkpoints, with no model calls.

6
00:01:35.878 --> 00:01:43.948
C1, message history: every runtime message is a card with sender, recipient, type and payload, tool calls included.

7
00:01:45.033 --> 00:01:48.807
Long payloads expand in place. The coloured bar marks the sending agent.

8
00:01:49.412 --> 00:01:59.560
After 228 messages the run ends without an answer. Expected: 6651.7, financial-year visitors divided by six instruments.

9
00:02:00.671 --> 00:02:05.723
Sending: a broadcast to the group or a direct message to one agent lands in the runtime queue.

10
00:02:06.029 --> 00:02:11.661
Run processes the queue continuously, Step processes one message, Drop discards the next.

11
00:02:12.297 --> 00:02:15.870
Dropped. The queue is empty again and the agents never saw the message.

12
00:02:20.983 --> 00:02:27.497
C3, the overview: one column per session, one rectangle per message. Legend buttons filter.

13
00:02:28.127 --> 00:02:33.709
Colour by sender: orchestrator, Coder, Executor, WebSearcher and user.

14
00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:36.499
Colour by recipient topic or agent.

15
00:02:37.121 --> 00:02:40.462
Colour by message type. Tool calls stand out from text.

16
00:02:41.072 --> 00:02:44.359
Hover shows the message. A click scrolls the history to it.

17
00:02:44.688 --> 00:02:47.091
The legend filter dims every other message type.

18
00:02:48.432 --> 00:03:01.657
C2. The mistake starts at message nine: the orchestrator's plan assumes a published financial-year report and forbids estimating. The observatory only publishes monthly tables, so every later instruction inherits the wrong plan.

19
00:03:03.184 --> 00:03:11.069
We edit the plan in place: the fact sheet and plan now point at the two monthly visitor reports, the financial-year definition and the facilities page.

20
00:03:13.224 --> 00:03:22.177
Reset-and-edit restores every agent from the checkpoint before message nine, re-publishes the edited plan and forks a new session. The old branch stays viewable.

21
00:03:22.811 --> 00:03:25.831
Step: the runtime processes just this one message.

22
00:03:31.038 --> 00:03:37.353
The edited broadcast is processed; the orchestrator's request-to-speak waits in the queue. Run continues the branch.

23
00:03:42.221 --> 00:03:55.534
The steered branch finished: with the plan rewritten, the same team sums the monthly figures and reaches 6651.7 instead of giving up after 228 messages. The paper's modify-the-plan strategy.

24
00:03:56.161 --> 00:04:02.617
The overview now shows both sessions. The amber marker is the fork point, and the session tabs switch between branches.

25
00:04:03.336 --> 00:04:06.553
The original failing branch is still browsable for comparison.

26
00:04:10.884 --> 00:04:11.867
Status. Fully working: runtime with per-message checkpoints, C1, C2, replayable recordings. Basic: the C3 overview. Harness: 29 tasks; 97 of 97 Opus runs succeeded, natural failures come from the Haiku team. Next: more failing runs, E1, study instruments and the pilot.
